        <div class="section-desc">Executive summary of findings and recommended strategic priorities for policy and funding decisions</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <ul class="takeaway-list">
      <li class="takeaway-item">
        <div class="takeaway-icon">&#128200;</div>
        <div>
          <h4>Open Access Is Becoming the Default — But the Transition Is Uneven</h4>
          <p>The OA rate rose from 64.1% (2024) to 74.1% (2025), a 10-point jump in one year. 8 of the 12 highest-volume journals are fully open access. <strong>Strategic implication:</strong> Funding mandates requiring OA publication are increasingly compatible with where researchers already want to publish. However, China — the world's largest producer — sits at only 55.5% OA. International OA frameworks should specifically address engagement with Chinese research institutions.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="takeaway-item">
        <div class="takeaway-icon">&#127758;</div>
        <div>
          <h4>China's Dominance Is Structural, Not Cyclical</h4>
          <p>With 22.1% of all publications — more than twice the USA's 10.7% — China is the unambiguous leader in biomedical output volume. <strong>Strategic implication:</strong> Research partnerships, citation networks, and funding collaborations that exclude or underweight Chinese institutions are operating with a significant blind spot. Portfolio diversity for funders should include explicit China-facing strategies.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="takeaway-item">
        <div class="takeaway-icon">&#129520;</div>
        <div>
          <h4>The Gut Microbiome Is a High-Priority Growth Area</h4>
          <p>Gastrointestinal Microbiome ranks as the 4th most frequent MeSH major topic overall, displacing longer-established areas like cardiovascular disease. This reflects a rapid maturation of the field since 2020. <strong>Strategic implication:</strong> Funding bodies should anticipate continued growth in microbiome-related grant applications. Reviewers with cross-disciplinary expertise (microbiology × gastroenterology × metabolism) will be in high demand.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="takeaway-item">
        <div class="takeaway-icon">&#129514;</div>
        <div>
          <h4>COVID-19 Research Persists as the Largest Single Topic</h4>
          <p>COVID-19 remains the most frequently assigned MeSH major topic (43 sample records; est. ~1,116 full dataset). Three years post-pandemic, continued investment in long COVID, pandemic preparedness, and immunological sequelae is reflected in the data. <strong>Strategic implication:</strong> Funders should distinguish between pandemic-reactive research (likely tapering) and pandemic-preparedness infrastructure research (which should be sustained).</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="takeaway-item">
        <div class="takeaway-icon">&#9888;</div>
        <div>
          <h4>MeSH Indexing Lag Distorts Emerging Topic Detection</h4>
          <p>2025 and 2026 records show dramatically lower MeSH major topic assignment rates — not because research is less topic-focused, but because recent articles haven't yet been fully indexed. <strong>Strategic implication:</strong> Any system that ranks emerging research areas using PubMed MeSH data will systematically undercount recent work. Policy analysts should complement PubMed data with preprint server monitoring (bioRxiv, medRxiv) for real-time topic surveillance.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="takeaway-item">
        <div class="takeaway-icon">&#128101;</div>
        <div>
          <h4>Team Science Is the Norm — Systematic Reviews Are an Exception Worth Watching</h4>
          <p>With a system-wide median of 6 authors and collaborative outputs from large consortia reaching 115 authors, solo-investigator biomedical research has effectively ceased to be the norm. However, Systematic Reviews — arguably the most policy-relevant output type — have the lowest OA rate (56.5%) of any type. <strong>Strategic implication:</strong> Policy pressure to make evidence syntheses open access should be a specific targeted mandate, not assumed to follow from general OA uptake.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="takeaway-item">
        <div class="takeaway-icon">&#128218;</div>
        <div>
          <h4>Preprints Signal Where Science Is Moving Fastest</h4>
          <p>With 100% OA rates and the highest median author count (8), preprints represent cutting-edge, large-team work entering the public record before peer review. bioRxiv appears in the top 12 journals by volume. <strong>Strategic implication:</strong> Funding evaluation panels should develop formal policies for how to treat preprint evidence — ignoring them creates a systematic lag between emerging science and funding decisions.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <div class="insight" style="margin-top:28px; font-size:14px; background:#f0f4f8; border-left-color: #3a5c8a;">
      <strong>Data Note:</strong> All findings in this report are derived from a 4,886-record analytical sample drawn from the full 126,832-abstract PubMed dataset (kanchana1990/biomedical-research-abstracts-20242026, Kaggle). Proportions and rates are directly from the sample; estimated full-dataset counts apply a scale factor of 25.96x. The sample shows consistent distributional properties across all measured dimensions. Temporal comparisons across years use rates, not raw counts, to account for indexing lag asymmetry.
    </div>
